<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">EOKA</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1955">1955</a>–<a href="/years/1959">1959</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Ethniki Organosis Kyprion Agoniston, National Organisation of Cypriot Fighters</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">EOKA, the National Organisation of Cypriot Fighters, was a Greek Cypriot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> organisation that fought British rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> from 1955 to 1959 with the aim of enosis, union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-grivas">George Grivas</a> and backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makarios-iii">Archbishop Makarios</a> III, it waged a campaign of bombings and ambushes that ended with the agreements creating an independent Republic of Cyprus in 1960.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eoka.jpg" alt="EOKA" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dahn · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EOKA_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> was conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in 1571, and control passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> on 4 June 1878 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1877-1878">Russo-Turkish War</a>. British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">censuses</a> recorded a Greek Cypriot majority throughout, with Turkish Cypriots the smaller community. The organisation was headed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-grivas">Georgios Grivas</a>, a Greek Army officer from Cyprus who had led the anti-Nazi and anti-communist resistance group Organization X in occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. He took the nom de guerre Digenis, after the Byzantine hero Digenis Akritas.</p><p>Grivas arrived to stay in early November 1954. He recruited from the Cyprus Farmers' Union in the villages and from two youth movements, the Church-controlled OHEN and the nationalist PEON, in the towns. The armed wing consisted of mountain groups in hidden camps and town groups, supported by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-front-czechoslovakia">National Front</a> of Cyprus that supplied intelligence and weapons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archbishop Makarios III and Grivas disagreed over method: Makarios wished to limit the campaign to sabotage without loss of life, while Grivas aimed at continual harassment of the British, believing that British repression would alienate the population and give <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> a case before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>. The armed struggle began just after midnight on 31 March to 1 April 1955 with eighteen bomb attacks, accompanied by a proclamation signed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-grivas">Digenis</a>. Grivas at first forbade attacks on Turkish Cypriots.</p><p>Governor John Harding arrived on 3 October 1955 and declared a State of Emergency on 26 November. After talks with Makarios failed, the British exiled him to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seychelles">Seychelles</a> on 9 March 1956, which brought a general strike and a sharp rise in attacks.</p><p>Violence between the communities grew as Grivas began targeting Turkish Cypriot policemen, and the Turkish Cypriot organisation TMT emerged. After the MacMillan Plan, which Greek Cypriots rejected, he cancelled a further truce on 7 September 1958 and ordered attacks on British people, in which eight were killed in the next two months. Makarios hinted that he would accept an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the Zurich and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> agreements Makarios became the first president of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and staffed the government and civil service with EOKA members. Extremists of both communities remained armed, and the new state collapsed after Makarios changed the constitution unilaterally. In 2012 the Foreign Office released documents in which colonial officials admitted torture and abuse between 1955 and 1959. The Republic of Cyprus regards EOKA's members as freedom fighters and marks 1 April as a national anniversary, while a Turkish Cypriot textbook calls the campaign barbaric and illegal. A memorial museum was created in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicosia">Nicosia</a> in 1960.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “EOKA” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Cyprus, Christodoulos Papachrysostomou set to it &quot;It Was the First of April&quot;, an <b>EOKA</b> song commemorating the start of the EOKA uprising against British colonial rule on 1 April 1955.</blockquote><a href="/songs/oh-my-darling-clementine">Oh My Darling, Clementine</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1955 the <b>EOKA</b> guerrilla movement under George Grivas, backed by Archbishop Makarios III, fought British rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-cyprus">British Cyprus</a></li></ul></section>
